The `ArrayUtils.add` method from `org.apache.commons.lang3` is used to add an element to an array. Here is the code snippet of the method:

```java
public static <T> T[] add(T[] array, T element) {
    Class<?> type;
    if (array != null){
        type = array.getClass();
    } else if (element != null) {
        type = element.getClass();
    } else {
        type = Object.class;
    }
    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked") // type must be T
    T[] newArray = (T[]) copyArrayGrow1(array, type);
    newArray[newArray.length - 1] = element;
    return newArray;
}
```

In the failing test `testLANG571()`, a `String[]` and a `String` are both initialized to `null`, and then `ArrayUtils.add` is called with these values:

```java
String[] stringArray=null;
String aString=null;
String[] sa = ArrayUtils.add(stringArray, aString);
```

The bug report mentions that when both `stringArray` and `aString` are `null`, the `add` method returns an `Object[]` array, which can lead to a `ClassCastException`.

To diagnose this issue, we can establish the following steps:
1. When both `stringArray` and `aString` are `null`, the `ArrayUtils.add` method should ideally handle this case to avoid returning an `Object[]` array.
2. The current behavior of `ArrayUtils.add` may lead to unexpected `ClassCastException` when both input parameters are `null`.
3. The method is not handling the case where both inputs are `null` appropriately, leading to the reported issue.

Next, we will suggest the location in the code that needs to be fixed.
The issue can be fixed by updating the `ArrayUtils.add` method in `org.apache.commons.lang3` as follows:

```java
public static <T> T[] add(T[] array, T element) {
    if (array == null && element == null) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Cannot add a null element to a null array.");
    }

    Class<?> type;
    if (array != null){
        type = array.getClass();
    } else if (element != null) {
        type = element.getClass();
    } else {
        type = Object.class;
    }
    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked") // type must be T
    T[] newArray = (T[]) copyArrayGrow1(array, type);
    newArray[newArray.length - 1] = element;
    return newArray;
}
```

The above change will throw an `IllegalArgumentException` if both `array` and `element` are `null`, preventing the creation of an `Object[]` array and avoiding the `ClassCastException`.

Therefore, the method that needs to be fixed is:

```java
org.apache.commons.lang3.ArrayUtils.add(T[], T)
```

